  "coordinateSystem": "wgs84",
  "vertexOrder": "[lon, lat] — GeoJSON order (x, y). NOT [lat, lon].",
  "accuracyHonesty": "⚠️ READ THIS BEFORE TREATING ANY BOUNDARY AS A LINE. Paul, on finishing the trace (2026-08-31): 'in this picture it looks like there are a lot of shadows and that made it very hard to be exact with positioning and borders, especially with the low resolution.' That is a REAL and EXPLAINABLE limit, not carelessness — a 10 January capture at 34.5N puts the sun near 32 degrees at noon, so every tree, wall and structure throws a long shadow, and leaf-off bare branches add shadow texture over open ground. At 0.6 m/px there is no detail underneath a shadow to recover. So: these polygons record WHERE A NAME APPLIES, at the resolution of a name. They are NOT survey lines, NOT parcel boundaries, and NOT a basis for anything that turns on where an edge actually falls. The real budget is the NAIP spec (+/-20 ft) plus relief displacement at 2,959 ft plus the shadow ambiguity above — call it worse than +/-30 ft on any edge Paul could not positively see.",
  "sharedBorders": "Adjacent zones were traced independently, by eye, with no vertex snapping. Neighbouring boundaries WILL disagree by a few feet and leave slivers and overlaps between them. Deferred deliberately (Paul, 2026-08-31): border smoothing and snapping come after the zones exist, not before. Do not read a gap between two zones as unclaimed ground.",
  "status": "PROPOSAL — NOT FOLDED. zones.json is unchanged. Folding is a separate reviewed act: it must decide the four open questions below, and dispose of the four old draft zones that have no counterpart here (Fairway, Parking Bank, Upper-Uber Wall Area, House). plants.json references zones by zoneId, so a fold that drops or renames an id breaks those references.",
